// A method's OWN type parameter that shadows one of the enclosing type's is a different `K`, and it
// resolves through a different channel: the receiver's reflected tag records the CLASS's arguments,
// the call node's `type_args` operand carries the METHOD's. Both reach the one compiled body, so
// the shadowing name answers with the method's instantiation.
//
// **Discriminating on purpose.** This case used to instantiate both `K`s with the same type `A`, so
// it read as proof of shadowing while being equally consistent with the opposite rule — and the
// opposite rule was what the checker actually implemented (the class's argument won a name-keyed
// substitution and the method's own `K` was unreachable). Here the class is a `Pair<A, int>` and the
// argument is a `B`: under the old behaviour this was `E0007`, `argument of type B is not assignable
// to A`. It answers `B` only because the two `K`s are genuinely two parameters.
//
// This was a refusal (E0058) while a method had no forwarding channel at all: the two `K`s were
// indistinguishable at run time, so answering either would have been a guess. Shadowing is now an
// ordinary scoping question with an ordinary answer, and the sibling case that still refuses —
// a parameter no call can infer — is `reflection/type_name_diagnostics.noe`.
// expect: stdout "1B"
// expect: stdout "1A"
// expect: exit 0
struct A {
    x: int
}
struct B {
    y: int
}
class Pair<K, V> {
    pub n: int

    fn new(n: int): Pair<K, V> {
        return Pair { n: n };
    }

    // `K` shadows the class's; it is inferred from the argument, not read off the receiver.
    fn shadowed<K>(x: K): string {
        return "${self.n}" ~ type_name::<K>();
    }

    // The class's `K`, reached under a name the method does not shadow — the control that proves
    // the receiver channel still works and that the two answers differ.
    fn own(): string {
        return "${self.n}" ~ type_name::<K>();
    }
}
p: Pair<A, int> = Pair.new(1);
echo p.shadowed(B { y: 2 });
echo p.own();
